<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Penkovka culture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Penkovka culture is an early Slavic archaeological culture of the 6th and 7th centuries, found in the forest-steppe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> and beyond. Its small settlements of sunken huts, hand-made pottery and metalwork are usually identified with the Antes known from Byzantine writers. It is named after a site near the village of Penkivka in central Ukraine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/penkovka-culture.jpg" alt="Penkovka culture" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Slovenski Volk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slavic_archaeological_cultures,_beginning_of_7th_century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
